                             than the top edge of the window?                   
                             \\n\\nNo wait. Let me set coordinates.             
                             Plane at altitude h above the center of            
                             the Earth. Let me put the observer at              
                             height h above point P on the surface              
                             (origin). The tangent line at P is the             
                             x-axis. The observer is at (0, h). The             
                             tangent point to the horizon is at                 
                             distance d from P along the surface.               
                             \\n\\nBy the Pythagorean theorem,                  
                             d\\u00b2 + h\\u00b2 = (R+h)\\u00b2 =               
                             R\\u00b2 + 2Rh + h\\u00b2\\nSo d\\u00b2            
                             = 2Rh + h\\u00b2\\nd = \\u221a(2Rh +               
                             h\\u00b2)\\n\\nFor the line of sight:              
                             the tangent segment from observer to               
                             tangent point makes angle \\u03b8 with             
                             the vertical (radial line). From                   
                             earlier, \\u03b8 = arccos(R/(R+h))                 
                             \\u2248 3.13\\u00b0 from vertical. So              
                             the line of sight to the horizon is                
                             3.13\\u00b0 from vertical, which means             
                             it\'s 86.87\\u00b0 from the local                  
                             horizontal (x-axis). That would mean               
                             the line of sight to the horizon goes              
                             DOWN at 3.13\\u00b0 from horizontal.               
                             \\n\\nWait, that can\'t be right for a             
