                             \\u03b1, -R + d sin \\u03b1 -                      
                             h)\\nVector from C to T: (d cos                    
                             \\u03b1, -R + d sin \\u03b1)\\nDot                 
                             product = 0:\\n(d cos \\u03b1)(d cos               
                             \\u03b1) + (-R + d sin \\u03b1 - h)(-R             
                             + d sin \\u03b1) =                                 
                             0\\nd\\u00b2cos\\u00b2\\u03b1 + (-R + d            
                             sin \\u03b1)\\u00b2 - h(-R + d sin                 
                             \\u03b1) = 0\\nBut (-R + d sin                     
                             \\u03b1)\\u00b2 = R\\u00b2 - 2Rd sin               
                             \\u03b1 + d\\u00b2                                 
                             sin\\u00b2\\u03b1\\nAnd                            
                             d\\u00b2cos\\u00b2\\u03b1 + d\\u00b2               
                             sin\\u00b2\\u03b1 = d\\u00b2\\nSo:                 
                             d\\u00b2 - 2Rd sin \\u03b1 + R\\u00b2 -            
                             hR + hd sin \\u03b1 = 0\\nSince the                
                             line passes through O and T, and T is              
                             at distance d from P along the                     
                             surface... actually d is the arc                   
                             length, so d\\u00b2 is the chord length            
                             squared only if \\u03b1 is the central             
                             angle.\\n\\nHmm, this is getting                   
                             complicated. Let me use the simpler                
