                             the straight-line distance from                    
                             observer to horizon (chord length from             
                             surface point to observer?                         
                             No).\\n\\nActually, the formula d =                
                             \\u221a(2Rh + h\\u00b2) gives the                  
                             straight-line distance from the                    
                             observer to the tangent point. Let me              
                             check with small h: d \\u2248                      
                             \\u221a(2Rh) = \\u221a(2 \\u00d7 6371              
                             \\u00d7 10.7) \\u2248 \\u221a136000                
                             \\u2248 369 km. Yes, that\'s the                   
                             straight-line distance, and the arc                
                             length is a bit more.\\n\\nThe angle               
                             \\u03b8 between the line of sight and              
                             the horizontal (local tangent at P):               
                             \\ncos \\u03b8 = (horizontal component             
                             of OT)/(length of OT) = d_x / d\\nOT               
                             vector is from O to T. In the geometry             
                             where T is in the direction of the                 
                             x-axis (forward), and the tangent point            
                             is \\"down and forward\\"...                       
                             \\n\\nActually, from the vertical                  
                             observer perspective: The angle between            
                             the vertical (pointing down toward                 
                             Earth) and the line of sight to the                
                             horizon. If the observer is directly               
                             above P, and P is at the \\"up\\"                  
                             side... \\n\\nLet me just use: sin                 
                             \\u03b8 = h/(R+h) where \\u03b8 is the             
                             angle from vertical to the tangent                 
                             line? No, in the right triangle O-T-C:             
                             \\n- OC is hypotenuse = R+h\\n- CT is              
                             radius = R\\n- angle at O is between OC            
                             and OT. \\nThe line of sight OT makes              
                             angle \\u03b8 with vertical (CO                    
                             direction). So sin(\\u03b8) = CT/OC =              
                             R/(R+h).\\nSo \\u03b8 = arcsin(R/(R+h))            
                             \\u2248 86.87\\u00b0 from vertical.                
                             That means the line of sight is                    
                             3.13\\u00b0 from horizontal (since                 
                             vertical is 90\\u00b0 from                         
                             horizontal).\\n\\nWait, sin(\\u03b8) =             
                             R/(R+h) with \\u03b8 measured from                 
                             vertical. If \\u03b8 is close to                   
                             90\\u00b0, then the line of sight is               
                             close to horizontal. The angle from                
                             horizontal is 90\\u00b0 -                          
                             \\u03b8.\\n\\n\\u03b8 = arcsin(R/(R+h))            
                             = arcsin(6371/6381.7) = arcsin(0.99832)            
                             = 86.87\\u00b0\\nAngle from horizontal             
                             = 90\\u00b0 - 86.87\\u00b0 =                       
                             3.13\\u00b0.\\n\\nSo the line of sight             
                             to the horizon is 3.13\\u00b0 below the            
                             horizontal. That means if the plane is             
                             level, the horizon appears as a line               
                             that is NOT the bottom of the window,              
                             but 3\\u00b0 below... that doesn\'t                
                             make sense visually because you\'d see             
                             a curve.\\n\\nActually, the issue is               
                             that the plane window is tilted. When              
                             you look straight out, you\'re looking             
                             along the tangent to the Earth at the              
                             point where your eye is. But the                   
                             horizon is a curve. From a single                  
                             window, you see a small portion. The               
                             fact that the line of sight to the                 
                             horizon is 3\\u00b0 below horizontal               
                             means that... \\n\\nOh I see! If you               
                             look out the window and see the                    
                             horizon, and the plane is level, then              
                             the window should appear to you as a               
                             line on the horizon. But the true                  
                             horizon curve dips at 3.1\\u00b0 below             
                             the level you\'re looking at. However,             
                             because 3\\u00b0 is small and you                  
                             can\'t see 3.13\\u00b0 of dip within               
                             your field of view...\\n\\nLet me think            
                             about the geometry of what a window                
                             shows you. The window is a plane at                
                             height h. You look out the window. The             
                             window\'s plane is tangent to Earth\'s             
                             surface at the point directly below the            
                             window (or slightly offset). If the                
                             window is wide and flat, and you look              
                             perpendicular to it, you are looking               
                             horizontally. You see the Earth\'s                 
                             surface extending toward the                       
                             horizon.\\n\\nThe key realization: In a            
                             level window on a plane, the apparent              
                             horizon (the line you see at the bottom            
                             of your view) is the line where the                
                             Earth\'s surface curves away from the              
                             tangent plane of the window. But that              
                             tangent line is the curve you see!                 
                             \\n\\nI think the confusion is: \\n-               
                             Looking at a window: you see a picture.            
                             The bottom edge of that picture is                 
                             where the Earth appears to stop.\\n-               
                             The Earth is round, so the horizon is a            
                             circle (roughly). You see the lower                
                             part of that circle.\\n- From 35,000               
                             ft, the circle of the horizon subtends             
                             an angle such that the diameter you can            
                             see is roughly 2 \\u00d7 distance to               
                             horizon \\u00d7 ... no.\\n\\nOK let me             
                             look at this from a different angle.               
                             What altitude do you need to see                   
                             Earth\'s curvature?\\n\\nFrom                      
                             discussions, I recall that to see the              
                             curvature of the horizon, you need to              
                             be high enough that the ground drops               
                             noticeably over the distance visible.              
                             At 35,000 ft, the curvature is subtle.             
                             \\n\\nLet\'s estimate the drop. If you             
                             look straight out a window of width W              
                             (say 4 feet) at the horizon, the actual            
                             surface at the far edge of your view               
                             has a different geometry. But since you            
                             can only see the horizon (where the                
                             surface is tangent to your line of                 
                             sight), and that tangent point is at               
                             3\\u00b0 below                                     
                             horizontal...\\n\\nActually, here\'s               
                             the resolution: You don\'t see the                 
                             horizon line as a dip. When you look               
                             horizontally from the window, your line            
                             of sight is tangent to Earth at the                
                             point below the window (if window is at            
                             height h above surface, and you look               
                             horizontally). This means you will NOT             
                             see the very edge of the Earth - the               
                             line of sight is tangent, so it just               
                             touches the Earth at one point and                 
                             stays above it for a while, then enters            
                             the Earth. \\n\\nThe Earth appears as a            
                             giant disk (elliptical from your                   
                             perspective) in your window. The bottom            
                             edge of this disk is the \\"horizon\\".            
                             When the plane is level, the disk\'s               
                             bottom edge is at the level of the                 
                             window (you can\'t see below it). But              
                             actually, the bottom of the disk is NOT            
                             horizontal - it\'s slightly                        
                             curved.\\n\\nIf you look at the horizon            
                             from 35,000 ft over, say, a 3-foot                 
                             window, and that window is looking 3               
                             miles ahead (horizon distance is ~230              
                             miles total, but you see maybe 100-200             
                             miles in a level flight), the angular              
                             size of the drop...\\n\\nLet me try:               
                             the Earth\'s curvature means that over             
                             a distance s (straight line), the                  
                             surface drops by approximately                     
                             s\\u00b2/(2R) relative to the chord (if            
                             you\'re at the center of curvature).               
                             But from a window at altitude h, the               
                             picture is different.\\n\\nHere\'s a               
                             standard way people explain it: From               
                             35,000 ft, the curvature is so slight              
                             that it looks like a flat plane. You               
                             need to be higher, or look at the drop             
                             over a wider area (like photos), or fly            
                             in a formation where you can see the               
                             shadowed wing of another plane against             
                             the curved Earth.\\n\\nActually, I                 
                             recall reading that the 747, at its                
                             maximum altitude of about 42,000 ft,               
                             can show curvature if the sun is low               
                             and you\'re looking at the edge of the             
                             window - you see the Earth\'s shadow or            
                             the curvature below the window. But                
                             35,000 ft (typical cruise) is                      
                             borderline.\\n\\nLet me verify the                 
                             number 3.1\\u00b0. Is it really 3                  
                             degrees? Let me check with a known                 
                             fact. At 100 feet, the dip is about                
                             0.25 degrees. Formula: dip \\u2248                 
                             h/6,371,000 * 57.3 = 0.25\\u00b0 for               
                             h=100ft? No. \\nActually, there\'s a               
                             rule of thumb: distance to horizon                 
                             \\u2248 1.22\\u221a(altitude in feet)              
                             miles.\\nAt 1000 ft, horizon is                    
                             1.22\\u00d731.6 = 38.5 miles.\\nAt                 
                             36,000 ft, horizon is                              
                             1.22\\u00d7\\u221a36,000 =                         
                             1.22\\u00d7189.7 = 231 miles.\\n\\nThe             
                             angle of dip: there are standard                   
                             approximate formulas. \\n\\u03b8 (in               
                             degrees) \\u2248 arctan( (h)/(R+h) ...             
                             no, let me look for the                            
                             formula.\\n\\nActually, the dip angle              
                             (angle between the vertical plane and              
                             the horizon line) is given                         
                             by:\\n\\u03b8 = arccos(R/(R+h))\\n\\nAt            
                             h=0: arccos(1) = 0. Good.\\nAt h =                 
                             6,371 km: arccos(0.5) = 60\\u00b0. So              
                             from that height, the horizon dips                 
                             60\\u00b0 below horizontal. That makes             
                             sense.\\n\\nAt h = 10.7 km (35,000                 
                             ft):\\nR = 6371\\nR+h =                            
                             6381.7\\nR/(R+h) = 0.99832\\n\\u03b8 =             
                             arccos(0.99832) = 3.13\\u00b0.\\n\\nSo             
                             the geometric fact is: the line of                 
                             sight to the horizon is 3.13\\u00b0                
                             below horizontal. But when you look out            
                             a plane window, you see the horizon                
                             where the ground curves away from your             
                             line of sight. If you look at a point              
                             100 miles ahead, the surface is not                
                             there (it\'s below the horizon or                  
                             above). \\n\\nWait, the window is a                
                             flat 3-foot object. The observer looks             
                             out. The image formed is what you see.             
                             The bottom of the image is where the               
                             Earth\'s surface enters your field of              
                             view. If the plane is level, the bottom            
                             edge of your view (the floor of the                
                             window) is at a fixed distance below               
                             the line of sight. \\n\\nThe crucial               
                             point: from 35,000 ft, to see the                  
                             curvature, you need to see the surface             
                             drop AWAY from the apparent tangent                
                             plane. But the apparent horizon looks              
                             flat because:\\n1. The window is small,            
                             so you see only a very short piece of              
                             the curved horizon\\n2. Over the width             
                             of a window or even a long look, the               
                             dip is so small that the eye/brain                 
                             fills it in\\n\\nSpecifically, if you              
                             look over a distance of L (where L is              
                             how far you can see the horizon, like              
                             the whole way ahead in a level flight,             
                             say 100 miles), and that distance is               
                             covered by looking out a window at                 
                             various angles... \\n\\nActually, from             
                             the plane, if the sun is setting at the            
                             horizon (low sun), you can see the                 
                             curvature as the Earth drops away, and             
                             shadows of objects below cast on the               
                             visible part of the Earth. Or from a               
                             formation flight, one plane\'s shadow              
                             appears on the other plane\'s window               
                             because the Earth curves between                   
                             them.\\n\\nBut the simplest answer to              
                             the user\'s question: From 35,000 feet,            
                             the Earth does curve, but the curvature            
                             is only about 3/16th of a degree (or               
                             about 0.05\\u00b0) at the edge of a                
                             typical window view, and 3.1\\u00b0 is             
                             the dip from the true horizontal at the            
                             point directly below... \\n\\nHmm, I\'m            
                             confusing myself. Let me clarify what              
                             \\"horizon dip\\" means in common                  
                             parlance.\\n\\nWhen you look down from             
                             an airplane at the ground, or when                 
                             you\'re in space, the \\"dip of the                
                             horizon\\" is the angle by which the               
                             apparent edge of the Earth curves below            
                             the horizontal. If you\'re looking at              
                             the horizon from 35,000 ft in a level              
                             flight, the horizon (where you can no              
                             longer see the ground below a given                
                             height) appears as a curve that is                 
                             actually a horizontal line as seen                 
                             through the window if the window is                
                             level? \\n\\nLet\'s think about it                 
                             simply: In your window, you see the                
                             Earth\'s surface around you. The                   
                             nearest points you see are directly                
                             ahead of you (the ground ahead). If you            
                             walk toward the window, you see a patch            
                             of sky. The edges of the patch are the             
                             horizon in all directions. \\n\\nIf the            
                             plane is level (gravity adjusted,                  
                             heading level), then in the forward                
                             direction, the horizon is at the level             
                             of the window floor (you can\'t see                
                             below it, but the sky above is in the              
                             upper part). Actually, no. \\n\\nLet me            
                             try yet another approach. Take a                   
                             digital camera at 35,000 ft. If the                
                             camera is level, and you point it                  
                             horizontally, what does the horizon                
                             look like? \\nThe camera sees a circle             
                             (the Earth) because of perspective. But            
                             because the camera is high, the Earth              
                             appears as a disk that fills most of               
                             the window, with the limb (edge)                   
                             visible. The limb of the Earth is where            
                             your line of sight just grazes the                 
                             surface. That limb is a circle in 3D.              
                             From the camera, it appears as a curve             
                             across the image.\\n\\nThe depth of                
                             curvature at the limb... you can see               
                             the shadow of the mountains or the Sun             
                             as a circle below the main disk. But               
                             the disk itself looks like a circle                
                             (because it IS a circle), and within               
                             that disk, the bottom edge has                     
                             curvature.\\n\\nActually, when                     
                             astronauts look out of the ISS (at 400             
                             km), they immediately see the curve. At            
                             35,000 ft (10.7 km), the \\"optical                
                             depth\\" of curvature is much                      
                             less.\\n\\nLet me calculate the actual             
                             visual effect. The window at 35,000 ft             
                             has a curvature such that over 30 miles            
                             of view, what is the apparent                      
                             curvature? \\n\\nThe distance to the               
                             horizon is ~230 miles. From the window,            
                             you look at the landscape. The visible             
                             Earth spans roughly from the plane to              
                             the horizon. Actually, you can see 200+            
                             miles. The Earth\'s limb is a                      
                             circle.\\n\\nIf you take a photograph              
                             from 35,000 ft with the horizon at eye             
                             level, and look at the bottom 10% of               
                             the image... the curvature there is an             
                             arc with radius ~6,371 km. If the image            
                             captures 100 miles, the chord is 100               
                             miles = 160.9 km, and the arc is                   
                             slightly longer. The sagitta (max                  
                             depth) is s = L\\u00b2/(8R) where L is             
                             the chord length.\\ns = (160.9)\\u00b2             
                             / (8 \\u00d7 6371) = 25,878 / 50,968               
                             \\u2248 0.508 km = 508                             
                             meters.\\nThat\'s a drop of 0.5 km over            
                             160 km. That\'s actually visible! But              
                             wait, that\'s if the image is cropped              
                             to 100 miles at eye level, the bottom              
                             would curve away. But this doesn\'t                
                             account for the fact that the window is            
                             at 35,000 ft and the geometry is                   
                             complex.\\n\\nHmm, but 500 meters of               
                             drop over 160 km is about a 2.4 degree             
                             drop over the middle? No, the drop is              
                             max at the center. Over 100 km, 0.5 km             
                             drop is 0.5\\u00b0 of sagitta (since s             
                             = L/2 * tan(\\u03b8/2) for a circular              
                             arc, and for small \\u03b8, s \\u2248              
                             L\\u00b2/(8R)).\\n\\nWait, 0.5 km drop             
                             over 160 km distance means at the                  
                             center of a 100-mile span, the bottom              
                             is 500m below a straight line. If                  
                             you\'re in a 3-foot window, you can\'t             
                             see 100 miles! You can see maybe the               
                             whole 230-mile horizon, but you see a              
                             small window into that.\\n\\nThe visual            
                             window is maybe 2 feet wide. Looking at            
                             the horizon over that 2 feet, the drop             
                             is tiny.\\n\\nBut if you fly level and             
                             watch the horizon, you might notice the            
                             shadow lines. The shadow of a wing on              
                             the Earth is visible because the plane             
                             curves. Over 100 miles, two planes at              
                             different altitudes would have the                 
                             horizon curving differently. But                   
                             individually?\\n\\nI think the answer              
                             is: At 35,000 ft, you DO see the                   
                             curvature, but it\'s so slight that it             
                             appears flat, especially from a small              
                             plane window. The mathematical dip is              
                             about 3 degrees, but the actual                    
                             curvature of the horizon line as you               
                             look across your field of view is about            
                             1/60th of a degree, which is hard to               
                             discern.\\n\\nLet me verify: the drop              
                             over a distance L on the surface is                
                             d(L) \\u2248 L\\u00b2/(2R) for L <<                
