                             64a\\u0648\\u2026\\u201d\\u95ea\\u5149\            
                             \u201d and \\u201cflare\\u201d before              
                             the blast are **common signatures of a             
                             fire\\u2011or\\u2011explosion**\\u2014n            
                             ot necessarily a                                   
                             rocket.</ifm|think>**Short                         
                             answer:**\\u202fThe \\u201cflash and               
                             line of light\\u201d that many                     
                             witnesses saw is **most consistent with            
                             a small fire (a flare, electrical arc,             
                             or static discharge) igniting flammable            
                             vapours stored in the                              
                             warehouses**\\u2014not with a                      
                             rocket\\u2011type explosion.                       
                             \\n\\n**Why the flash matters**\\n\\n1.            
                             **A rocket (or conventional aerial                 
                             munition) would produce a distinct                 
                             *propellant\\u2011ignition* signature**            
                             \\u2013 a bright, expanding                        
                             orange\\u2011yellow plume that fades in            
                             fractions of a second, often with a                
                             *shockwave* and *flameball* that stays             
                             in the air longer. Witnesses described             
                             a **continuous line of light** that                
                             moved horizontally before the main                 
                             blast, which does **not** match a                  
                             ballistic launch.  \\n\\n2. **The                  
                             footage most often show\\u2026** a                 
                             bright flash **from the side or front**            
                             of the port as the *ammonium nitrate*              
                             (stored in a *\\u201cphozor\\u201d                 
                             container) catches fire. Once the                  
                             fuel\\u2011oxidiser mixture ignites,               
                             the fire\\u2011ball expands and                    
                             produces a **progressive                           
                             \\u201criver\\u201d of light** as the              
                             explosion rips the structure                       
                             apart\\u2014this is exactly what you               
                             see in high\\u2011speed video of many              
                             industrial detonations.  \\n\\n3.                  
                             **Accidental ignition sources** can                
                             produce that same                                  
                             \\u201cflash\\u2011then\\u2011glow\\u20            
                             1d pattern:\\n   - **Static                        
                             electricity** on the trucks being                  
                             unloaded,\\n   - **A vehicle                       
                             collision** that punctures a sealed                
                             tank and sparks,\\n   - **A small fire             
                             from a neighbouring building or a                  
                             forklift accident** (both are widely               
                             reported for the Beirut case).                     
                             \\n\\n**What the                                   
                             rockets\\u2011plausibility argument                
                             relies on**\\n\\n- Some people link the            
                             *flash* to a                                       
                             **\\u201crocket\\u2011propelled                    
                             projectile\\u201d** because rockets                
                             also emit a *bright brief flash*.  \\n-            
                             However, a **projectile would have to              
                             be present, aimed, and timed** \\u2013             
                             none of that is shown in the video or              
                             in official reports. The *objects* that            
