                             (if h=0). The surface curves away. The             
                             maximum curvature effect across the                
                             field of view is tiny.\\n\\nLet\'s step            
                             back. The user\'s question is likely               
                             based on a flat-earth misconception:               
                             \\"At 35,000 ft, if Earth were round, I            
                             should see the curve.\\" The correct               
                             explanation is that 35,000 ft is very              
                             close to the surface; the curvature is             
                             only detectable at higher altitudes or             
                             over long baselines. The atmosphere                
                             also bends light and hides the                     
                             curvature.\\n\\nWe should provide:\\n-             
                             A quick calculation of Earth\'s radius             
                             and the ratio.\\n- The concept of                  
                             angular resolution and scale.\\n-                  
                             Mention that from ISS, the curvature is            
                             visible (you see the \\"sail\\" of                 
                             Earth), and from high mountains (like              
                             Everest) you also see it, though it\'s             
