                             angle formula, showing it\'s small at              
                             35k ft, but you need to look at a                  
                             larger angular field or use a telescope            
                             for high-altitude \\"curvature\\".\\n-             
                             Also note that clouds and light                    
                             pollution obscure the line of                      
                             sight.\\n\\nWe can also use the analogy            
                             of a large sphere: like looking at a               
                             beach ball from a small distance; it               
                             looks like a flat disk. The curvature              
                             is only obvious if you measure the                 
                             distance from center to                            
                             edge.\\n\\nLet\'s structure the                    
                             answer:\\n\\n1. Acknowledge the                    
                             observation: yes, the horizon looks                
                             flat.\\n2. Explain why it\'s \\"flat               
                             enough\\": 35,000 ft is only 0.17% of              
                             Earth\'s radius (R = 6,371 km, altitude            
                             = 0.011 km, ratio ~0.0017). The local              
                             geometry is dominated by the huge                  
                             sphere.\\n3. Explain the math: dip                 
                             angle \\u2248 \\u221a(2h/R) \\u2248                
                             3.3\\u00b0 \\u2014 small. Over the                 
                             width of a cabin (a few meters), the               
                             Earth\'s surface drops only                        
                             micrometers. Even over 100 km, the drop            
                             is a few meters, but you can\'t see                
                             that from a jet because the atmosphere             
                             cuts off the line of sight before you              
                             see that far.\\n4. Clarify that from               
                             35k ft, the Earth actually fills the               
                             sky; the horizon is far (230 miles                 
                             away), but the top of the                          
                             atmosphere/cloud layer is what you see,            
                             not the solid Earth. You can\'t measure            
                             the 3.3\\u00b0 dip without knowing the             
                             distance.\\n5. When is curvature                   
                             visible? At high altitude (ISS, space              
                             station ~400 km), the horizon is much              
                             lower relative to the window and the               
                             curvature is apparent; or if you drive             
                             across the continent, you see the drop.            
                             From a jet, the drop over the miles                
                             visible is small.\\n6. Mention the                 
                             \\"flattening\\" of the window vs the              
                             curvature: A window is a flat plane;               
                             the horizon is a circle, but the eye               
                             cannot discern the curvature at this               
                             scale.\\n\\nAlso, we can address the               
                             \\"obvious\\" part: It\'s not about the            
                             dip angle alone; it\'s about the ratio             
                             of the curvature radius to the viewing             
                             distance. The curvature of a circle is             
                             1/R. If R is 6,371 km, then the sagitta            
                             per meter is 1/(2R) = 8e-8 m. So to see            
                             the curvature, you need to either go               
                             very high (so that the effective radius            
                             is smaller relative to the window) or              
                             have a very long viewing baseline (like            
                             driving). From a jet, the effective                
                             baseline is limited by the cabin and               
                             the atmosphere.\\n\\nI should also                 
                             mention that the horizon is essentially            
                             a circle in the sky; if you could look             
                             at the entire angular distance to the              
                             horizon (which is ~200+ degrees? No,               
                             the full disc is about 180 degrees.                
                             Actually, from 35k ft, the Earth fills             
                             the entire sky, so the horizon is all              
                             around, but the \\"flatness\\" is                  
                             because the local surface is locally               
                             Euclidean to human scale.\\n\\nWait, if            
                             Earth fills the entire sky, then the               
                             horizon is not a distinct line in front            
                             of you; the Earth and sky blend. You               
                             see the blue, then white clouds, then              
                             beyond that the night sky. The                     
                             curvature of the outline is not                    
                             resolvable because the outline is                  
                             essentially the entire                             
                             hemisphere.\\n\\nThus the answer: At               
                             35k ft, you are inside the Earth\'s                
                             \\"looking ball\\" but very close to               
                             the surface. The Earth looks like a                
                             huge sphere; the curvature is below the            
                             threshold of human vision at that scale            
                             and altitude.\\n\\nLet\'s double-check             
                             the dip angle calculation: h = 35,000              
                             ft = 10.668 km. R = 6371 km. \\u03b1               
                             (central angle) = arccos(R/(R+h)) =                
                             arccos(6371/6381.7) = arccos(0.99834) =            
