                             That\'s not huge compared to Earth\'s              
                             circumference (40,000 km). So you\'re              
                             seeing a small slice of the Earth\'s               
                             surface. Over that slice, the curvature            
                             is subtle. Wait, the drop of 11 km is              
                             not subtle in absolute terms, but                  
                             relative to 370 km it\'s 3%. Visually,             
                             if you look at the \\"horizon\\" you               
                             are seeing the sky; you don\'t see the             
                             drop because the atmospheric scattering            
                             makes the sky go to space                          
                             quickly.\\n\\nAnother way: The human               
                             eye is not a CCD sensor that measures              
                             angles to the horizon. To detect                   
                             curvature, you need to see the outline             
                             of the sphere against a background.                
                             From a jet, the sphere fills the sky,              
                             so the outline is not a clean circle;              
                             it\'s a dome of sky with horizon lines             
                             near it. The curvature is not directly             
                             visible. If you went to, say, 10,000               
                             meters and looked at a clear valley,               
                             you might see the curvature at the far             
                             edge of the valley over a long                     
                             distance.\\n\\nThe standard answer:                
                             \\"From 35,000 feet, the curvature is              
                             too small to be perceived because                  
                             Earth\'s radius is huge relative to the            
                             altitude. You would need to be at                  
                             orbital altitude (like 160 km) to see              
