                             telescope to resolve the 10-degree                 
                             arc.\\"\\n\\nWait, at 35k ft, the                  
                             horizon dip is 3.3\\u00b0, which is                
                             visible if you have a perfect flat                 
                             window and no atmosphere. But in                   
                             practice, the eye is not a                         
                             inclinometer. The atmosphere diffuses              
                             the light. And the human eye cannot                
                             easily perceive a 3\\u00b0 angular                 
                             difference unless there are two                    
                             reference points. The difference in                
                             color (blue sky vs Earth) at the                   
                             horizon could help. But the                        
                             \\"flatness\\" perception is because               
                             the horizon appears as a line, and we              
                             don\'t know the radius of                          
                             curvature.\\n\\nI think the best answer            
                             focuses on scale: The ratio of altitude            
                             to radius is 0.17%. The curvature drop             
                             over a typical window width is                     
                             nanometers. The dip angle is only about            
                             3.3\\u00b0, but the line of sight is               
                             not a straight tangent; you look at the            
                             edge and can\'t resolve the difference.            
                             To see it, you must go to a much higher            
                             altitude where the dip angle is much               
                             larger (e.g., ~10-20 degrees from a                
                             plane\'s perspective) and the Earth\'s             
                             disk is smaller, allowing you to                   
                             measure the curvature                              
                             directly.\\n\\nActually, let\'s                    
                             compare: At 35k ft, the angular size of            
                             the Earth as seen by the entire window?            
                             The Earth fills the entire sky above               
                             the horizon? No, the Earth is below                
                             you. The window shows the sky. The                 
